WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.356
Welcome to the ten-minute rapid review of innate immunity.

2
00:00:04.406 --> 00:00:10.585
We cover the top pearls and traps from all six modules, then build one master map.

3
00:00:10.967 --> 00:00:15.996
Keep one rule in mind: the lost function predicts the disease.

4
00:00:16.046 --> 00:00:20.830
Lose an early classical component, and you often get lupus.

5
00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.206
Lose C3: pyogenic infections.

6
00:00:23.256 --> 00:00:25.255
Lose C5 to C9: Neisseria.

7
00:00:25.633 --> 00:00:26.280
Module 1.

8
00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:32.391
Innate receptors are germline-encoded and nonclonal, with fewer than 100 types.

9
00:00:32.441 --> 00:00:34.325
Innate memory is limited.

10
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:38.998
Three phases: immediate innate, zero to four hours.

11
00:00:39.048 --> 00:00:43.005
Early induced innate, four to ninety-six hours.

12
00:00:43.055 --> 00:00:45.819
Adaptive, after ninety-six hours.

13
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.565
Barriers come first: keratin, tight junctions, mucus and cilia.

14
00:00:51.615 --> 00:00:54.488
Paneth cells make alpha-defensins.

15
00:00:54.538 --> 00:00:56.379
LL-37 neutralizes LPS.

16
00:00:56.429 --> 00:01:01.278
In cystic fibrosis, thick mucus leads to lung infections.

17
00:01:01.633 --> 00:01:06.052
Trap: lysozyme works better on Gram-positive bacteria.

18
00:01:06.102 --> 00:01:10.023
In Gram-negatives, LPS covers the peptidoglycan.

19
00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:19.779
TNF, IL-1 and IL-6 cause fever, leukocytosis and acute-phase proteins.

20
00:01:19.829 --> 00:01:22.877
CRP can rise 1000-fold.

21
00:01:23.233 --> 00:01:26.332
TNF mediates much of septic shock.

22
00:01:26.382 --> 00:01:31.148
But trap: anti-TNF gave no benefit in sepsis trials,

23
00:01:31.198 --> 00:01:35.315
and TNF blockade can reactivate tuberculosis.

24
00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:46.841
For allergy: epithelial IL-25, IL-33 and TSLP drive ILC2s, Th2 cells and mast cells.

25
00:01:46.891 --> 00:01:49.514
IL-33 is an alarmin.

26
00:01:49.867 --> 00:01:51.308
Module 2, the cells.

27
00:01:51.358 --> 00:01:56.455
Neutrophils are the most abundant white cells, and they arrive first.

28
00:01:56.505 --> 00:02:00.632
Azurophilic granules hold myeloperoxidase and defensins.

29
00:02:00.682 --> 00:02:02.124
They also cast NETs.

30
00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:07.288
Abbas says most tissue macrophages are fetal-derived and self-renew.

31
00:02:07.338 --> 00:02:09.991
Janeway says they come from monocytes.

32
00:02:10.041 --> 00:02:13.619
CD47 binding SIRP-alpha is the don't-eat-me signal.

33
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:19.923
Plasmacytoid DCs are the major type I interferon source.

34
00:02:19.973 --> 00:02:24.190
cDC1 cells cross-present to CD8 T cells.

35
00:02:24.567 --> 00:02:31.506
NK cells are CD56 positive, CD3 negative.

36
00:02:31.556 --> 00:02:39.860
Low MHC class I removes inhibition: missing self.

37
00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:43.952
CD16 binds IgG for ADCC.

38
00:02:44.333 --> 00:02:48.586
NK deficiency, for example GATA2 or CD16, gives

39
00:02:48.636 --> 00:02:53.072
severe herpesvirus and papillomavirus infections.

40
00:02:53.433 --> 00:03:00.093
Trap: tryptase, not histamine, marks mast cell activation.

41
00:03:00.143 --> 00:03:06.224
C3a, C5a and MRGPRX2 activate mast cells without IgE.

42
00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:12.993
Trap: eosinophils have very low FcεRI without a signalling chain.

43
00:03:13.043 --> 00:03:15.669
IL-5 is their key cytokine.

44
00:03:15.719 --> 00:03:19.832
Galectin-10 forms Charcot-Leyden crystals.

45
00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.595
Module 3, sensing.

46
00:03:22.645 --> 00:03:26.535
Location predicts the ligand.

47
00:03:26.585 --> 00:03:33.328
Surface TLRs 1, 2, 4, 5 and 6 see microbial walls.

48
00:03:33.378 --> 00:03:39.713
Endosomal TLRs 3, 7, 8 and 9 see nucleic acids.

49
00:03:40.067 --> 00:03:45.084
LPS binds TLR4, with MD2 and CD14.

50
00:03:45.134 --> 00:03:48.214
Flagellin binds TLR5.

51
00:03:48.264 --> 00:03:52.835
Double-stranded RNA binds TLR3.

52
00:03:52.885 --> 00:03:55.667
CpG DNA binds TLR9.

53
00:03:56.033 --> 00:04:02.087
Classic trap: TLR3 is the only TLR without MyD88.

54
00:04:02.137 --> 00:04:07.941
Only TLR3 and TLR4 use TRIF, so TLR4 uses both.

55
00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:14.373
MyD88 or IRAK4 deficiency: early invasive pneumococcal

56
00:04:14.423 --> 00:04:19.135
infection with no fever, a cold infection.

57
00:04:19.185 --> 00:04:25.711
TLR3, UNC93B or TRIF defects: herpes simplex encephalitis.

58
00:04:26.067 --> 00:04:31.703
NLRP3 senses urate crystals, ATP and potassium efflux.

59
00:04:31.753 --> 00:04:35.704
Caspase-1 cleaves pro-IL-1β and IL-18.

60
00:04:35.754 --> 00:04:44.129
Gasdermin D pores cause pyroptosis, in macrophages and DCs, but not neutrophils.

61
00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:49.322
NLRP3 gain of function causes CAPS: FCAS with cold-induced

62
00:04:49.372 --> 00:04:54.195
fever and urticarial rash, and Muckle-Wells with deafness.

63
00:04:54.245 --> 00:04:57.639
Abbas reports that IL-1 antagonists work.

64
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.138
Trap: weak NOD2 variants raise Crohn's disease risk.

65
00:05:02.188 --> 00:05:05.601
NOD2 gain of function causes Blau syndrome.

66
00:05:05.967 --> 00:05:07.800
Module 4, phagocytes.

67
00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:12.283
Recruitment runs: selectin rolling on sialyl Lewis

68
00:05:12.333 --> 00:05:16.946
X, integrin activation, firm arrest, transmigration.

69
00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:24.990
LAD-1, a CD18 defect: leukocytosis, no pus, poor wound healing.

70
00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:30.518
LAD-2: no sialyl Lewis X, Bombay blood group.

71
00:05:30.568 --> 00:05:35.187
LAD-3: bleeding with normal platelets.

72
00:05:35.567 --> 00:05:40.357
Trap: LAD means leukocytosis in blood but no pus in tissue.

73
00:05:40.407 --> 00:05:43.639
Do not confuse it with severe congenital

74
00:05:43.689 --> 00:05:47.824
neutropenia, below 0.5 times 10 to the 9 per litre.

75
00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:55.652
Respiratory burst: NADPH oxidase makes superoxide, which becomes hydrogen peroxide.

76
00:05:55.702 --> 00:05:59.539
Myeloperoxidase then makes hypohalous acid.

77
00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:04.554
CGD breaks the oxidase; two-thirds is X-linked gp91.

78
00:06:04.604 --> 00:06:09.258
Expect catalase-positive Staphylococcus, granulomas,

79
00:06:09.308 --> 00:06:13.238
and Aspergillus, the leading cause of death.

80
00:06:13.288 --> 00:06:15.500
MPO deficiency is milder.

81
00:06:15.867 --> 00:06:20.234
Chédiak-Higashi, from LYST mutations: partial

82
00:06:20.284 --> 00:06:25.535
albinism, silver hair, giant lysosomes, weak NK cells.

83
00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:36.450
Disseminated BCG or Salmonella means MSMD: the IL-12 and IFN-γ axis fails.

84
00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:38.950
Module 5, complement.

85
00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:41.359
C3 cleavage is central.

86
00:06:41.409 --> 00:06:47.643
Classical and lectin pathways share the C3 convertase C4b2a.

87
00:06:47.693 --> 00:06:51.309
The alternative pathway uses C3bBb.

88
00:06:51.667 --> 00:06:57.160
Trap: for C2, the larger bound fragment is C2a.

89
00:06:57.210 --> 00:07:00.344
Factor D circulates active.

90
00:07:00.394 --> 00:07:03.529
Properdin stabilizes C3bBb.

91
00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:08.312
Only antigen-bound antibody activates C1.

92
00:07:08.362 --> 00:07:16.691
IgM beats IgG; IgG1 and IgG3 are best, and IgG4 does not activate complement.

93
00:07:17.067 --> 00:07:23.144
C5a is the most potent anaphylatoxin, and degranulates mast cells without IgE.

94
00:07:23.194 --> 00:07:28.093
The membrane attack complex lets water in, and the cell bursts.

95
00:07:28.467 --> 00:07:29.752
The brakes.

96
00:07:29.802 --> 00:07:35.579
Factor H regulates only the alternative pathway.

97
00:07:35.629 --> 00:07:37.400
C4BP binds C4b.

98
00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:41.770
DAF dissociates both C3 convertases.

99
00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:43.591
CD59 blocks C9.

100
00:07:43.967 --> 00:07:44.902
Module 6.

101
00:07:44.952 --> 00:07:54.096
C1q, C2 or C4 deficiency gives lupus in over half; C2 deficiency is the most common.

102
00:07:54.146 --> 00:07:59.788
C3 or factor I deficiency gives pyogenic infections.

103
00:08:00.167 --> 00:08:05.621
C5 to C9, properdin or factor D deficiency gives Neisseria.

104
00:08:05.671 --> 00:08:08.699
Properdin deficiency is X-linked.

105
00:08:08.749 --> 00:08:10.845
MBL deficiency is mild.

106
00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:16.441
Factor H or MCP mutations cause atypical HUS.

107
00:08:16.491 --> 00:08:19.145
PNH lacks CD59 and DAF.

108
00:08:19.195 --> 00:08:25.964
Trap: the PIG-A mutation in PNH is somatic, not inherited.

109
00:08:26.333 --> 00:08:30.831
Hereditary angioedema is autosomal dominant C1 inhibitor

110
00:08:30.881 --> 00:08:34.647
deficiency, and bradykinin causes the swelling.

111
00:08:34.697 --> 00:08:40.087
Clues: abdominal attacks, no wheals, no response to antihistamines.

112
00:08:40.467 --> 00:08:47.801
The 2017 guideline says: test C1 inhibitor function, protein, and C4.

113
00:08:47.851 --> 00:08:51.867
HAE type 1: low function, low protein.

114
00:08:51.917 --> 00:08:56.683
Type 2: low function, normal or high protein.

115
00:08:57.033 --> 00:09:01.564
Acquired angioedema starts later, with lymphoma

116
00:09:01.614 --> 00:09:05.268
or MGUS, and C1q is low in 75 percent.

117
00:09:05.318 --> 00:09:10.044
ACE inhibitor angioedema has normal C1 inhibitor.

118
00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:11.345
HAE traps.

119
00:09:11.395 --> 00:09:13.634
C3 and CH50 are normal.

120
00:09:13.684 --> 00:09:17.714
Normal C1 inhibitor does not exclude HAE.

121
00:09:17.764 --> 00:09:20.998
C4 is not useful below 12 months.

122
00:09:21.048 --> 00:09:23.585
Wheals do not exclude HAE.

123
00:09:23.967 --> 00:09:28.638
Treatment follows the 2017 guideline; check the current one.

124
00:09:28.688 --> 00:09:32.572
Treat attacks early with C1 inhibitor, ecallantide

125
00:09:32.622 --> 00:09:36.270
or icatibant, not tranexamic acid or androgens.

126
00:09:36.633 --> 00:09:43.317
For long-term prophylaxis, C1 inhibitor is first line, androgens second.

127
00:09:43.367 --> 00:09:46.403
Carry medication for two attacks.

128
00:09:46.453 --> 00:09:48.367
Avoid ACE inhibitors.

129
00:09:48.733 --> 00:09:50.186
Now the master map.

130
00:09:50.236 --> 00:09:52.796
Barriers fail in cystic fibrosis.

131
00:09:52.846 --> 00:09:57.778
Sensing fails in cold infections, herpes encephalitis and CAPS.

132
00:09:57.828 --> 00:10:01.257
Cells fail in severe congenital neutropenia.

133
00:10:01.633 --> 00:10:09.582
Effector mechanisms: phagocytosis fails in LAD, CGD and Chédiak-Higashi.

134
00:10:09.632 --> 00:10:13.915
Complement: lupus, Neisseria, HAE, PNH.

135
00:10:13.965 --> 00:10:18.359
Inflammation: septic shock, IL-1 excess.

136
00:10:18.409 --> 00:10:21.025
NK cells: herpesviruses.

137
00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:24.578
Last, the link to adaptive immunity.

138
00:10:24.628 --> 00:10:26.372
Antigen is signal 1.

139
00:10:26.422 --> 00:10:30.856
Innate responses give signal 2: costimulators like

140
00:10:30.906 --> 00:10:35.250
CD80 and CD86, cytokines and complement products.

141
00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:36.954
Failure here: MSMD.

142
00:10:37.333 --> 00:10:39.159
Five things to remember.

143
00:10:39.209 --> 00:10:42.130
Innate receptors are germline-encoded.

144
00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:44.397
Location predicts the ligand.

145
00:10:44.447 --> 00:10:46.820
Lost function predicts disease.

146
00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:48.931
HAE is bradykinin-mediated.

147
00:10:48.981 --> 00:10:51.354
Innate immunity gives signal 2.

148
00:10:51.733 --> 00:10:56.683
One last trap: TLR3 is the only TLR without MyD88.

149
00:10:56.733 --> 00:11:00.482
Review the master map before the exam.

150
00:11:00.532 --> 00:11:01.482
Good luck!
